<ArticleLayout slug="facebook-post-specs" ctaLabel="Create your account" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">There are three answers to the Facebook post size question in 2026, because Meta writes three sets of rules. Its ads guide sizes paid placements. Its developer pages set the limits for a page post sent by a publishing tool. And for a photo you post by hand, Meta prints no pixel size at all.</p>

  <p>Reviewed October 11, 2026, against the Meta Ads Guide, the Meta Business Help Center, the Facebook Help Center and Meta for Developers. Every value on this page comes from one of those four.</p>

  <h2>What size is a Facebook post in 2026?</h2>

  <p>Build a feed image at 4:5, 1,440 by 1,800 pixels: that's the size Meta's ads guide asks for in Facebook Feed. A page post sent through Meta's API, which is how a tool such as hubStudio publishes, takes photos of 10 MB at most. Link images want 1.91:1, at 1,200 by 630 or larger.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta's ads guide asks for a 4:5 image at 1,440 by 1,800 pixels in Facebook Feed, JPG or PNG, up to 30 MB, at least 600 pixels wide and 750 tall.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Ads Guide, Awareness image ad specs on Facebook Feed, read October 11, 2026. The page is undated; values quoted as printed.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A photo published to a Page through Meta's Graph API can be JPEG, BMP, PNG, GIF or TIFF and can't exceed 10 MB. A PNG should stay under 1 MB or it may look pixelated, and Facebook resizes every image to several dimensions.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta for Developers, Page Photos reference, read October 11, 2026.</p>

