  <p>So when one file has to serve a page post and an ad, build it to the organic limit, and it will fit the ad too.</p>

  <p>On shape, Meta's two ad pages agree. The Business Help Center recommends 4:5 for single-image ads in Facebook Feed, the same pick as the ads guide. That isn't true next door: for Instagram Feed the same help page says 1:1, which the <a href="/resources/insights/instagram-post-sizes-2026">Instagram post sizes for 2026</a> page covers.</p>

  <h2>What size should a Facebook Reel be?</h2>

  <p>Vertical 9:16. Through Meta's API that means 1,080 by 1,920 recommended and 540 by 960 at least; a Reels ad wants 1,440 by 2,560. Length is where most size guides go wrong. Meta changed it in 2025.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"All videos posted to Facebook will be shared as reels, regardless of length or orientation." Stories and Live are the exceptions. Meta's announcement said reels on Facebook would no longer have any length or format restrictions.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Facebook Help Center, "What are reels on Facebook" and "Create a reel on Facebook," update dated September 2025; Meta newsroom, June 17, 2025. Both read October 11, 2026.</p>

  <p>So the 90-second reel limit printed across the web no longer applies to a reel posted in the Facebook app. It still applies to one sent by a tool.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A reel published to a Page through Meta's Reels Publishing API must be 9:16, at least 540 by 960 pixels (1,080 by 1,920 recommended), 24 to 60 frames a second and 3 to 90 seconds long. A Page can publish 30 reels through the API in a rolling 24 hours.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta for Developers, Reels Publishing API, read October 11, 2026.</p>

