  <p>The Story row hides a small contradiction. The specification table on Meta's Stories API page says 3 to 90 seconds; the limitations list on the same page says a video story can't exceed 60. Build to 60. Instagram's own Reels and Stories figures differ again, and the <a href="/resources/insights/instagram-reels-stories-specs">Instagram Reels and Stories specs</a> page lays them out.</p>

  <h2>What is the safe zone on a Facebook Story?</h2>

  <p>Meta prints two different answers, both on its ads guide.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta's Facebook Stories video ad page asks for roughly 14 percent (250 pixels) at the top and 20 percent (340 pixels) at the bottom to stay free of text and logos. Its Facebook Stories image ad page asks for 14 percent at the top, 35 percent at the bottom and 6 percent on each side.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Ads Guide, Awareness video and image ad specs on Facebook Stories, read October 11, 2026. Figures quoted as printed on each page.</p>

  <p>The video page's percentages and pixels don't match each other, either. On a 1,080 by 1,920 frame, 14 percent is about 269 pixels, and 250 is closer to 13 percent; 340 pixels is about 18 percent, not 20. Facebook Reels ads, image and video, carry the 14, 35 and 6 set with no conflict.</p>

  <p>The safe course is the stricter set. On a 1,080 by 1,920 Story that means roughly 269 pixels clear at the top, 672 at the bottom and 65 on each side (our arithmetic on Meta's percentages). Meta's Business Help Center adds two warnings: on screens taller than 9:16 it may zoom the creative and crop outside the safe zone, and a Reels ad carrying a disclaimer should leave the bottom 40 percent clear. All of this is ad guidance. For organic Stories, Meta prints no safe zone.</p>

  <h2>What size is a Facebook link preview image?</h2>

  <p>1,200 by 630 pixels, at 1.91:1. Meta's developer page on link shares gives the numbers outright.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta asks for a shared-link image of at least 1,200 by 630 pixels for the best display on high-resolution screens, and at least 600 by 315 for a link post with the larger image. A smaller image still shows, "but the size will be much smaller." Keep it close to 1.91:1, at 8 MB or less and at least 200 by 200.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta for Developers, Images in Link Shares, read October 11, 2026.</p>

  <p>One practical rule from the same page: Facebook caches the image by its address. Replace the picture under the same URL and the preview won't change. Give the new image a new address, then run the link through Meta's Sharing Debugger so it's fetched before the first share.</p>

  <h2>How do Facebook carousels work?</h2>

  <p>Every published number is on the ad side. A Facebook Feed carousel ad takes 2 to 10 cards, square, at 1,080 by 1,080 or larger. The Business Help Center recommends keeping every card at one ratio, 1:1 or 4:5, so Meta doesn't adjust them for you, and reserves 4:5 carousels for catalog ads.</p>

  <p>A Facebook Stories carousel ad takes 3 to 10 cards and shows one to five of them on its own before the viewer asks for more. Its spec page recommends a 1:1 ratio and a resolution of at least 1,080 by 1,920 in consecutive lines. Those two can't both hold, so check the preview in Ads Manager before building a batch.</p>

  <p>For organic page posts, Meta's publishing documentation states no cap on photos per post.</p>

  <h2>How to make each Facebook format in hubStudio</h2>

  <p>The Facebook module in the hubStudio app publishes page posts, not profile posts: text only, one image, a carousel of 2 to 8 slides when rendered, or one video. A post carries pictures or a video, never both. It takes up to 10 pictures, JPG, PNG or GIF up to 10 MB each, the same ceiling Meta's API sets, or one MP4 or MOV clip of up to 20 minutes. Keep a PNG under 1 MB if you can; Meta warns that a heavier one may look pixelated.</p>

  <p>Each picture on a post opens in the Image editor, which crops it to Link (1.91:1) or Square, among its other tools. The clip opens in the Video editor on its Social panel, already set to a Facebook Reel, since Facebook shares every video as a reel. Pick Reel (9:16 at 1,080 by 1,920, marked as the best choice), Story, Feed portrait (4:5) or Square, then Apply the format. The editor shows in red what Facebook covers, draws the safe area in a dashed line, and checks the length and the file's weight against the network's limit. Editing in either one is free.</p>

  <p>Publish now or schedule the post, to up to 20 pages at once. Publishing to Facebook through hubStudio costs nothing; a render is charged to your prepaid balance at the price shown before you run it. The <a href="/app/image-tools">Image editor page</a> shows the tools in full, the <a href="/solutions/platforms/meta">Meta platform page</a> covers the studio's Facebook and Instagram work, and the <a href="/services/design/social-media">social media design service</a> is the route when you'd rather have the posts made for you.</p>

