  <h2>Questions about Facebook post sizes</h2>

  <h3>What size is a Facebook post image in 2026?</h3>

  <p>For a Facebook Feed ad, Meta's ads guide asks for 4:5 at 1,440 by 1,800 pixels, up to 30 MB. For an organic page post, Meta publishes no pixel size and resizes every image itself; through its API, a photo can't exceed 10 MB. Build at 1,440 by 1,800, keep the file at 10 MB or less, and one picture serves both.</p>

  <h3>What is the best aspect ratio for Facebook feed posts?</h3>

  <p>4:5, vertical. Meta's ads guide recommends it for Facebook Feed images and video, and its Business Help Center says the same for single-image ads. Square 1:1 is supported too, and it's the ratio Meta's ads guide asks for in a Feed carousel ad. Link images are the exception, at 1.91:1.</p>

  <h3>What size should a Facebook Reel be?</h3>

  <p>9:16. Meta's Reels Publishing API recommends 1,080 by 1,920 pixels and refuses anything under 540 by 960, with a length of 3 to 90 seconds. A reel posted in the Facebook app has had no length restriction since Meta's 2025 change. A Reels ad runs at 1,440 by 2,560 with no maximum length.</p>

  <h3>What is the safe zone on a Facebook Story?</h3>

  <p>Meta's ads guide gives two versions. Its Stories image ad page says to leave 14 percent at the top, 35 at the bottom and 6 on each side free of text and logos. Its Stories video ad page says 14 percent (250 pixels) at the top and 20 percent (340 pixels) at the bottom. Use the stricter set. Organic Stories have no published safe zone.</p>

  <h3>What size is a Facebook link preview image?</h3>

  <p>1,200 by 630 pixels at 1.91:1, according to Meta's developer page on link shares. The large link card needs at least 600 by 315; anything smaller shows much smaller. The file can't exceed 8 MB or fall below 200 by 200. Change the image's address when you replace it, or Facebook keeps showing the old one.</p>

  <h3>How many pictures can a Facebook post have?</h3>

  <p>Meta's publishing documentation sets no cap for an organic page post. Ads do have one: a Facebook Feed carousel ad takes 2 to 10 cards, a Stories carousel ad 3 to 10. Through hubStudio's Facebook module, a post carries up to 10 pictures, JPG, PNG or GIF, up to 10 MB each.</p>
